   "body": "## Pull request overview\n\nFixes an issue in snippet completion range calculation where, in replace-mode and with surrounding auto-closing pairs, the computed replace range could extend past the intended closing bracket and delete the following character.\n\n**Changes:**\n- Tightens the condition that decides when to “eat” a closing character from an auto-closing pair by incrementing `endColumn`.\n- Uses `snippet.prefix.endsWith(...)` and adds an additional guard intended to prevent overwriting past the closing bracket.\n\n<details open>\n<summary>Show a summary per file</summary>\n\n| File | Description |\r\n| ---- | ----------- |\r\n| src/vs/workbench/contrib/snippets/browser/snippetCompletionProvider.ts | Adjusts auto-closing-pair range extension logic for snippet completions to avoid unintended overwrite past a closing bracket. |\n</details>\n\n## Copilot's findings\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Comments suppressed due to low confidence (1)</summary>\n\n**src/vs/workbench/contrib/snippets/browser/snippetCompletionProvider.ts:159**\n* Please add a regression test for the replace-range behavior this block is adjusting (e.g. snippet prefix \"()\" with existing text \"()!!\" and cursor between the brackets should not extend the replace range beyond the closing bracket). There are already tests for replace ranges/auto-closing consumption, but this specific scenario doesn’t appear to be asserted.\n```\n\t\t\t\t\t// and make sure the endColumn isn't already overwriting the closing bracket\n\t\t\t\t\tsnippet.prefix.length !== p.open.length + p.close.length\n\t\t\t\t)) {\n\t\t\t\t\t// Eat the character that was likely inserted because of auto-closing pairs\n\t\t\t\t\tendColumn++;\n```\n</details>\n\n\n- **Files reviewed:** 1/1 changed files\n- **Comments generated:** 1\n\n\n",
